WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.780
John Elway's quarterback.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.120
Now strap on your helmet and go for it!

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:28.140
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.240
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.620
I'm Sean. I'm Joe.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:36.980
And I'm Sam. Guys, who is John Elway's quarterback?

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.100
It's not John Elway.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.900
Who is his quarterback?

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.180
Currently, his quarterback...

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.420
I failed at the joke.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.580
I don't even know who the quarterback is.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:52.420
Well, you can't even remember if he's the owner or not, so...

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.440
He's the real quarterback for the friends we made along the way.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.560
That's not true.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:57.780
That's not how quarterbacks work.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:59.540
I just think it's interesting.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.520
The game is called John Elway's quarterback.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.020
It should be like quarterback John Elway is the name of the game, right?

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:10.100
Yeah. I guess they're going for, you know, like the game is called quarterback and it's John Elway's game.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.240
Like, this is John Elway's quarterback.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.920
I think that would just be like John Elway colon quarterback.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.200
No, that implies that he owns the quarterback.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.080
Yeah. Yeah. Or that he is the quarterback.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:23.720
Well, I mean, he was a quarterback.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.200
Right. But I think that that's not what they're trying to say on this title.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.820
So that'll do it for today's episode.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:33.560
And you know what?

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:48.880
Since some weird things are already happening on the title screen in regards to the name, it's also worth pointing out that on the title screen, they're performing a lateral play on the title screen because the ball is going like across the field goal.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:57.500
So we can assume that it's just being thrown from like, you know, the side of the field to to a further back part of the field.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.640
Right, Sean? That's how a lateral works.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.660
Well, the ball is being thrown sideways.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.760
So it would have to be a lateral play.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.760
You don't throw the ball, you know, in a passing play.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:21.360
It could also be a trick of perspective because there are a lot of throws that go to the sidelines and depending on where you're viewing it from, it could look like a lateral.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:27.280
Also, laterals can look like forward passes like what happened in 1999 in Tennessee.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.520
Well, Sean, one thing I'm just I'm just going to throw this out here.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:37.180
OK, the field goal post, we're looking at it straight on and we're seeing the football.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.560
Remember, it's a 2D game.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:44.020
We are seeing the football completely flat going horizontally from the left to the right.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.660
So there is not really much up.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.160
What about this? It could be a really bad kick.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.120
Yeah, that's also true.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:58.360
You know how like before a while ago they had the I forget what they're called, like the lines that you can kick from were wider.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:02:59.960
Hash marks. The hash marks.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:05.640
Yeah. And I don't know if it was like that when this game came out, but maybe they were like all the way on the left.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:13.560
You know, and then while all the way on the left, the kicker accidentally like puts a ton of spin on it.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:18.700
So it gets that perfect spiral as it's being kicked through the air completely sideways.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.340
See, I think that you guys are overcomplicating.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.060
I think what's really happening is that there are just two football fields.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.620
One is perpendicular to the other.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.000
And we're watching it go, you know, go regularly across one of the fields.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.120
And we just happen in the background.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.560
We see the field goal of the perpendicular field.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:35.520
Totally different field.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:36.740
Not related to the game.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.400
I love that.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.860
Yeah, I appreciate that you're thinking outside the box right there.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:48.820
Right. Well, since I'm already picking on the game, let's just let's just walk through this game as if you're, you know, you're playing it for the very first time.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.890
You're at the team selection category.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:57.720
Right. And they can't they cannot license any teams or anything like that, even though they could get John Elway to sign his name.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.040
They couldn't get the NFL involved.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.980
There is no rhyme or reason to the team selection.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.620
If you pass your team, you can't just go back.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:07.100
Right. Like you say you want New York.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.820
Right. You see San Francisco, New York, Chicago.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.020
It's like, oh, no, no, wait.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.060
I want to New York. You have to go all the way through again, no matter which button you press.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.680
I thought Sesame Street.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.720
Yes. They do the Sesame Street one, two, three thing.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:20.840
It popularized it.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.100
That's funny. I thought that I was just making a mistake every time.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.020
What got me was that you can't hit start to select it.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.000
You have to hit A.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:33.000
And when you hit start, it's like the same thing as hitting the next like the right button.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.500
So I was like, did I did I do it?

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.040
Am I picking my opponent now?

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:43.860
Well, the funny thing, and I'm sure like it's pretty obvious, but it doesn't it doesn't matter like what team you pick.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.100
There's no difference in color of the uniforms.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.900
There's no difference in like what the field looks like.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:05:01.580
It's just literally a different city name at the top of the screen where the scoreboard is, which is insane to me that they would even put cities in here.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.340
Just make it red versus blue.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:09.040
But having grown up in New York your entire life, don't you feel obligated to pick New York?

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:12.480
Are you really going to go ahead and be like, ah, even though the stats don't matter, I'm going to be Chicago.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.320
Because I don't want to go through the whole title screen.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.360
I actually picked San Diego.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:19.620
That's very interesting.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.300
You want to explain a little bit behind your pick?

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.180
I don't know.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:26.220
I like, you know, I don't like the Jets.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.980
And so I don't want to play as New York.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:35.880
So I wanted to play as a team that no longer exists, which was the San Diego Chargers.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.840
I mean, you could have been the Giants.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:40.940
I don't really care about the Giants either, though.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.720
Where's the other New York team?

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.100
No, that's the Buffalo team.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:51.700
Oh, okay. You could be them with the Konami code.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:54.980
You get Buffalo. I would be honored.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.100
All right. So let's get to kickoff, okay?

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:10.560
You know, the game starts.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:17.180
I'm sure it determines whether you're the receiving or the kicking in, but I didn't remember watching that.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:18.660
I think you just automatically receive.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:19.880
I think so.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:20.940
There's no coin toss, right?

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.440
I think player one always receives.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:25.260
Okay, sure. So that's how it worked for me.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:30.060
And immediately from the kickoff, I could just tell something, okay?

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:39.340
So, like, we talked about Tecmo Bowl, and that's a game that treats football in a, like, 45-degree angle, horizontal playing field.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:51.060
This one goes back to a vertical, like, 10-yard fight style where everything is just flat, and it all happens in the same kind of relative position to each other.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:07:00.480
I really don't like the way these guys are running at the very start of the kickoff, and then I knew that I was just going to have a hard time running in general in this game.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:25.340
I'm not sure if I'm feeling the same way you are, but the weird thing that I felt about this game is that during kickoff, because the screen is sort of panning with you or panning with the ball, whoever you or your opponent is playing as will get, like, a huge speed boost.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:26.800
You become the Flash.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.480
Yeah, because they can't, like, render you off the screen.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.640
It's funny, because I didn't notice that until the fourth quarter.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.000
And then I was like, what the heck is going on?

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:38.520
I thought I noticed that the first time.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:47.180
So I'm hitting people back at, like, the 15-yard line because of this weird quantum mechanics.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:56.540
Overall, I didn't really feel too bad about, like, the direct one-to-one control of players.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.040
But I guess we can get into that.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:06.240
Well, yeah, because it's just like, for me, with the running, the way that they move, first off, does not simulate someone running to me.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:07.780
It's like a march, you know?

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:18.880
But even just the way that your characters move and stuff like that, a running play for me, nine times out of ten, resulted in a loss of yards.

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:22.800
And I don't think I ever gained more than three yards on a running play.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:24.460
So we're talking about offense now.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:27.860
I'm just talking about the way these characters move.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.560
It just never was efficient to be moving as characters.

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:38.360
Like, whether you're defense or you're offense, the way these guys move is like this weird high knees running in place.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.880
I'm glad you mentioned it, too, because I couldn't put my finger on it.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:47.480
I don't even know if I was having the same exact problem with the motion as you were.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:52.060
But right away, I was like, this feels not great.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:53.420
Like, something about it.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.800
I got used to it, actually, after a while.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.280
And then it was better. But right off the bat, I was a little bit...

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:00.440
Maybe it's because we just played, what, two other football games pretty recently?

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:04.900
That this one just felt so much more like a trudge to me.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:08.920
Yeah, the running for me was not good.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:14.640
I did not make any progress at all during any of the running plays that I tried to use.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:15.460
Well, here's the thing.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:24.440
Here's the thing. True to the name of John Elway's quarterback, the only person that can do anything with the ball is the...

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.440
Did I say John Elway's running back?

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:27.640
No, you said quarterback.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.580
Okay. The only person that can do anything with the ball is the quarterback.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:39.380
You don't get to hand it off to your running back like any modern NFL team would.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:41.560
It's just the quarterback.

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:51.740
So, like, if there was some distance that you could put between you and defenders by that handoff, you might have been able to get some movement.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:59.800
But I guess just because the name of the game is quarterback, they just decided not to program in a standard running game.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:02.440
They're all pass plays or QB sneaks.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:03.940
It's crazy. Yeah.

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:08.980
And even the pass plays, I feel like I was most successful.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:18.540
I just picked shotgun every single time because I feel like it was the only one that gave me enough space to get my pass ready and to have enough time to aim it and throw it.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:23.940
Well, you probably just found a way to, like, exploit against a computer there, right?

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:29.900
Like, in the sense of, like, you found a play that worked for you and the computer couldn't figure you out.

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:32.020
So you were like, I'm just going to keep playing shotgun, right?

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:39.020
I guess so. But I also feel like I tried the other plays and I feel like I just got, like, I got blitzed every single time.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:55.420
Yeah, I mean, while it does sound like the game wasn't really doing its job against the shotgun in particular, because I usually went shotgun or bomb or I forgot deep throw, whatever it's called.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:11:04.500
But, like, that is a part of the reason that that formation is designed that way is to give the quarterback more time.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:10.400
So it's both, like, a failure on the game's part and also the design of football in general.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:22.340
I guess, but I feel like because of the mechanics of the game that require you to, like, move your quarterback out of harm's way and then stop moving so that you can take the time to aim,

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:29.160
which, you know, takes a bit of time to move the cursor over the, you know, the guy you're trying to pass to,

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:35.040
I feel like you need as much time as possible to have it be successful.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:45.140
Yeah, I agree. There was something that was translated wrong, you know, into, like, what it would be like to actually, you know, to actually, like, look for your target.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:53.820
And, like, I mean, just to explain real quick what it's like to pass, so what Sam's getting at here is, like, when you hold A or B or whichever button it is to pass,

00:11:53.960 --> 00:12:02.500
and then a little cursor comes right out right at where your quarterback is, and you have to literally move the cursor along the field to the spot that you want to throw it in.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:08.580
So, like, that takes real time where you're just standing still moving your cursor to the distance you want to throw it.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:16.600
When you compare it to, like, Tecmo Bowl, where you're not aiming it, you're just selecting which receiver you can see is open.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:25.080
I mean, that, I think, is a lot quicker of a thing to do, but I feel like the game works that way.

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:40.940
Like, it uses that, like, quickness and, like, you're anticipating only having a split-second decision where this one feels like you should have more time because it takes more time to do the action of making a good throw.

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:59.820
This is sort of, I guess, where I know that there are huge problems with this system, but I am kind of impressed with this passing system because, yeah, with, like, Tecmo, it's a scrolling, like, cursor that, like, you select your receiver,

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:03.300
and then when you throw the ball, the trajectory is just calculated for you.

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:12.640
I really like the way that they did passing in this game where you can't, like, you have to aim at your guy.

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:22.780
And that also isn't just a downside of, like, this takes longer because you have to physically get the cursor to your intended receiver,

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:31.760
but it also allows you to, like, lead your guy away from coverage or just throw away a ball that would otherwise get intercepted.

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:35.540
There are actually incompletions in this game, which Tecmo didn't really have.

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:43.320
I think it's a really unique method, which, like, I don't think I've seen before or since.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:45.300
There might be other versions of this.

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:54.620
But I do agree that it's a little weird that you're sort of giving up the quarterback's control

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:59.160
because you can still move as the quarterback while you're selecting.

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:05.220
It's just, it either, like, locks in, like, what direction you were running when you started holding the button down

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:11.740
or it's, like, a one-to-one, like, your guy will move in the same direction as where you're aiming.

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:16.120
But it is something that, like, I did get the hang of.

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:23.500
But, yeah, I think it's both kind of a weird design but also a really interesting one.

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:26.860
Yeah, Sean, I think you brought up a lot of good points there.

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:28.960
I didn't hate the passing system.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:33.900
However, it needs to be mentioned, you know, usually I save this kind of stuff for sequels and spinoffs,

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:38.920
but it needs to be mentioned that this is a way of trying to figure out how to translate an arcade game

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:41.400
that is a very specific controller.

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:43.200
Yeah, I did read about that.

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:48.700
Right, it just can't be, like, they're not going to sell that accessory for this one game.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:55.280
So in the arcade version, which was originally just called Quarterback, it had no John Elway attachment to it,

00:14:55.760 --> 00:15:01.080
they had a spring-loaded joystick that controlled how far you throw and how you kick,

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:02.840
which I guess we'll talk about kicking later.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:09.440
But, you know, that spring mechanism makes for really fast, impulsive decisions about how far you pull back

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:12.680
and then, like, you know, you still see the arrow on screen,

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:16.040
but it's not like you're controlling it with a D-pad where you're trying to be accurate.

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:20.180
You're actually just trying to make sure you don't give too much pressure or too little pressure

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:23.220
that you throw a weaker, too far ball, which sounds great.

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:28.320
It just doesn't translate here on the NES because they're asking you to use the D-pad

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:32.240
and make it more about locating the precision of your player.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:37.800
To Sean's point, if you don't worry about the precision, it still will actually work pretty well, right?

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:42.480
Like, you can throw it kind of, you can throw a lob or you can kind of throw, like, a little further out,

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:44.820
and they will try to, like, make an effort to catch that play.

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:45.920
Yeah, the receivers will come back to it.

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:47.060
Yeah. Right.

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:48.180
So it's cool.

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:53.440
Yeah. What you're describing as the arcade sounds like, you know, like, the perfect solution.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:55.140
Well, I guess it wouldn't be a solution because it came before,

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:57.360
but the perfect, you know, solution to the problem we're having.

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:05.000
That sounds like the happy medium to me of, like, of making it doable,

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:12.340
but also, like, matching a little bit or simulating, rather, the, like, the difficulty of actually aiming.

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:16.240
So it's not just like, okay, select, okay, you'll automatically throw it directly to that receiver,

00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:18.880
where it's like you have to, there's actually some skill involved,

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:25.820
but it's not, like, taking up all your time where you have to suddenly stand still to, in order to do it.

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:34.660
Yeah. And I think that the aforementioned, like, running and general movement problems may have been, like, their fix for that,

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:40.980
because the game, I'm sure that the arcade version, I guess I'm not sure that it did this,

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:47.160
but I would assume that the arcade version moves a bit faster because you will have this quick release as the quarterback,

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:53.480
and they kind of had to slow everybody down just to make it, like, usable at all.

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:58.520
That's at least my, that's my theory on it.

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:02.660
But I do appreciate that they didn't throw the concept away,

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:10.140
like, the concept of, like, a more organic throwing system than just select receiver and throw.

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:16.500
Because I think what little personality this game has really just stems from that.

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:21.720
Yeah, I'd love to see if Madden ever did come to PCs.

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:22.820
It's not, right, Sean?

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:24.120
Like, you can't play Madden.

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:27.200
I think Madden is on EA Origin or something right now.

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:30.320
Oh, okay. I thought that was, like, for Xbox consoles only or something like that.

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:34.480
But if it is on PC, then they should add this, where, like, you know,

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:38.460
if you're doing mouse and keyboard, finally, like, you could use the mouse,

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:45.100
a super precise thing to decide where to throw to, you know, very quick and responsive.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:54.420
I will admit that, like, the D-pad's not exactly the most elegant way to locate another player that you want to pass to.

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:55.940
A mouse would be a lot better.

00:17:56.180 --> 00:18:00.000
But they didn't start adding mouses to Nintendo consoles until the Super Nintendo.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:06.120
That's true. All right, so we talked a lot about the passing here, and the rushing was just kind of like, you know,

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:08.800
we don't want to do it because you're the quarterback all the time.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:13.460
But we haven't talked about that there is a good amount of plays here to choose from,

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:16.300
and that was something we complained about in Tecmo Bowl.

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:20.560
And I think that, like, you know, the more options were there, and it wasn't that weird.

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:25.400
And I'm sure Tecmo Bowl could add more options in the future because it worked just fine here,

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.620
having multiple different plays to choose from.

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:35.800
Yeah, I appreciated that there were actual, like, real football concepts in the play structure here,

00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:38.620
and especially on the defense.

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:44.020
I mean, defense, well, we can get into the AI here, but defense is kind of a crapshoot.

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:51.320
But at least the play selection that you had wasn't just, like, rock, paper, scissors like Tecmo was.

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:58.760
Like, they were actually situational, like goal line defense, prevent defense, coverage, and blitzing.

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:04.440
I like that they added some of that sort of authenticity in there.

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:08.680
So I guess I didn't have anything else to say on that.

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:10.120
And that's fine.

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:12.740
We don't always need to comment on those things, but it's true.

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:21.980
It's like, you know, as somebody who doesn't know every football play there is, this felt like the right amount of things.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:26.820
Like, there was variety in the plays, but it wasn't, like, six pages of playbook options.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.080
You know, it made appropriate decisions for you.

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:33.320
And I think there's actually more decisions when you're playing defense, right?

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:38.920
Um, there's six plays on defense and nine on offense.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:40.580
Oh, okay. So I have it backwards.

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:42.200
Never mind. All right.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:45.680
So you're talking about the AI, and I do want to hear how everybody's games went.

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:59.660
But just to make my gameplay experience short, I learned very early on that they calculate things a little differently here in terms of where the play ends in this game of football.

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:05.780
Because if you dive, which in this game is incredible, you dive several yards.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:11.000
Yeah. Um, you, you know, you're able to get, like, a whole nother, like, four or five yards out of a dive.

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.360
If you dive, it's wherever you land with the ball.

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:18.400
That's, like, the, that's the part where we'll pick up the play.

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:20.500
You know, it doesn't even matter if somebody tackled you or something.

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:24.780
Like, as long as you dive for it, um, that's where the ball will resume.

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:27.100
So always dive at the end of a play.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:33.340
If you ever feel like anyone's even getting remotely close to you, you'll probably just be better off diving to get a couple extra yards.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:35.420
And that was my entire gameplay experience.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:38.400
I somewhat agree with that, that you should always dive.

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:41.900
Because that definitely gives you, uh, yeah, it gives you, like, several yards.

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:46.140
But I think that there's actually, there's actually merit to not diving.

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:50.640
Because I feel like the, uh, the defense misses very easily.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:58.200
Like, it's really easy to, like, just kind of shift from just going, like, because of, what are they, what are they, like, eight points of direction?

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:00.160
Or whatever, 16 points of direction?

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:04.140
You can shift, like, one of those degrees, like, to diagonal.

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:05.940
And they will, they'll miss tackling.

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:09.600
And, like, honestly, that's how I, like, scored most of, like, my touchdowns.

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:11.940
Is it just, like, ridiculous?

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:14.980
You just go from 45 degrees to the left to 45 degrees to the right.

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:17.960
And you'll, you'll get a bunch of them to jump.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:24.800
Yeah. And you could just cover, like, you could, you could, like, get them all to, like, dive and miss, you know, after you.

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:27.120
And then you're like, well, now there's nobody left.

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:30.860
Like, they're all, I mean, you know, obviously get back up and keep chasing you.

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:35.960
But, so, like, sometimes if I was, like, I was, like, absolutely doomed, I would, I would dive.

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:40.340
But, like, a lot of times I would just take my chances and see if, like, oh, you know, it's a good chance that they'll just miss.

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:42.100
Yeah. All right.

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:49.640
Let's talk about kicking because this was a weird one where there's no meter for the kicking.

00:21:49.880 --> 00:21:55.100
You would expect this to work similar to how, like, every NES golf game works where there's, like, a little power to it.

00:21:55.180 --> 00:22:00.140
And you got to try to adjust for, you know, the wind or centering of it.

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:02.660
It's very hard to miss a kick in this game.

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:04.200
Am I wrong? Yeah.

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:12.780
It basically, yeah, just basically spawns the cursor of, like, where the kick is aimed slightly to the left or slightly to the right.

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:21.980
And you just have to have non-brain damage reaction time to move it over to the center.

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:25.300
It's really hard to miss a kick here.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:28.180
Yeah. I actually laughed out loud when I first saw that.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:33.020
It was just, like, it was, like, the first kick, it was, like, just put the arrow where you want to kick it.

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:37.700
Like, you just decide to, like, if you want to kick a field goal or not, basically.

00:22:37.940 --> 00:22:42.940
I mean, yeah, you could, like, miss if you, like, waited too long, but it gives you plenty of time.

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:45.220
Kickers also got a crazy leg.

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:47.460
Like, the ball just keeps going.

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:50.880
Yeah, they just get a new ball after that.

00:22:50.880 --> 00:23:00.060
One thing I wanted to mention about the AI, if I may, of the game of, like, regular offense and defense.

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:12.740
It seems like they didn't really program any situational AI of, like, what play to select.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:20.520
Because I've, I got the computer to fourth down many times.

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:23.100
And, obviously, the fourth down is the last one.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:28.600
Usually people punt there if they're 20 yards away from their own end zone.

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:31.600
But every single time, they always went for it.

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:34.440
They were always trying to go long.

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:36.360
And I usually just sacked them.

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:46.160
I found that it was very easy to just, like, select the blitz play, line the linebacker up at this one certain spot.

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:49.300
And you have, like, a free pass to the quarterback.

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:52.540
And I got, like, a safety per quarter that way.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.980
Basically, I'm just saying that the AI in this game is jank.

00:23:58.900 --> 00:24:02.480
And I believe that because it was not even close.

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:08.860
In any games I played, the AI did not, uh, did not really stand a chance against me.

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:12.240
And, uh, my diving, say what you will, Joe.

00:24:12.580 --> 00:24:16.080
Uh, it, it just basically always sailed me to touchdowns.

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:19.840
Which weirdly have no music whatsoever when you get a touchdown.

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:22.420
Just a little jig. Yeah, yeah, sure.

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:23.440
But, like, I don't know.

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:26.200
There's not enough flair in this game already.

00:24:26.260 --> 00:24:28.980
And then, like, the touchdown's not really, like, a big to-do.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:30.600
But there's no halftime show.

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:32.600
Well, you don't like, da-da-da-da-da-da.

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.780
Yeah, I was gonna say, we do get that every single play.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:37.080
Yeah. I don't know if I need that.

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:39.560
Sometimes, like, I feel like you just get it in the middle of the play.

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:41.780
Yeah, it's random. Like, it's not, nothing's happening.

00:24:43.340 --> 00:24:44.520
But there's a halftime show.

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:46.480
Oh, there is? It says halftime.

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:48.740
Well, it says halftime. And there's a little song that plays.

00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:50.260
No, that's not a halftime show.

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:52.360
There's black screen, halftime, and white text.

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:54.420
And white goes, like, da-da-da-da-da-da-da or whatever.

00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:55.620
Yeah. Halftime show.

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:56.780
They should do that for the Super Bowl.

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:02.480
You could try to say, like, oh, well, you know, Mike's been found out.

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:04.080
He didn't even play, like, half this game.

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:06.540
That's just not true. I saw that as well.

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:10.080
And I made a note to say that there is no halftime show based on seeing that.

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:13.620
Like, based on what I saw, I deemed no halftime show.

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:17.220
For the record, I don't seriously consider that the halftime show.

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:18.680
You were being facetious.

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:21.620
Yes. I don't know.

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:27.640
You know, ever since the SpongeBob halftime show at the Super Bowl, it seems like, you

00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:29.840
know, halftime shows are up for debate if they're even real.

00:25:29.980 --> 00:25:31.340
It's a touchy subject. Right.

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:32.800
Yep. All right.

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:34.660
I don't really have much else to say.

00:25:34.660 --> 00:25:36.260
I was going to do a whole thing about John Elway.

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:38.300
Do you guys want anything else you want to talk about for this game?

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:40.740
I've got some defense thoughts.

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:42.460
Please. We don't really talk about defense.

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:47.760
But, no, I came up with a strategy for this game.

00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:51.920
And, Sean, I know you're like our resident football expert, but move over because I've

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:52.780
solved football.

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:57.600
Yeah. I solved football with a strategy on defense, at least.

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:02.240
Don't play. Because I'm not kidding.

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:05.200
Just one time I was just like, oh, like, I'm kind of tired of this game.

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:07.420
Like, after, like, it was like second quarter.

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:14.400
And I was like, I just, like, put the controller down for, you know, for like, for one dry.

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:16.140
Or, you know, they were just, they had the ball.

00:26:16.240 --> 00:26:17.520
I just put the controller down for a little bit.

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:22.060
And I did this for five of their possessions I just didn't play.

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:24.220
Three of them, I got an interception.

00:26:26.140 --> 00:26:30.860
The end of the game, from halfway through the game on, I stopped playing on defense.

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:33.060
I won the game 42 to 6.

00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:36.220
Without me playing at all, they only scored one touchdown.

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:37.820
And they missed the kick.

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:42.880
The AI is terrible in this game.

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:44.120
Yeah. Unless it's your AI.

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:51.860
Did you ever get a situation where your team will basically chase the quarterback off the screen?

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:58.240
And, like, since I guess it, like, doesn't register tackles off screen, it just, like, gets stuck there?

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:01.400
No. What happens?

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:08.960
You just got to restart? I basically had to, because I was, my character that I was controlling was drop back in coverage.

00:27:09.140 --> 00:27:15.860
So I had to run across the map to, like, the top of the screen where the quarterback was just somewhere.

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:20.920
And just sort of wait for his sprite to, like, peek his toe out.

00:27:21.020 --> 00:27:23.840
And I was finally able to tackle him.

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:26.060
It took, like, 30 seconds.

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:27.540
It was crazy.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:28.920
That's really fun.

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:32.980
I'd really love to see that happen, like, in a football game, like, on TV.

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:34.320
Like, he goes, like, out of frame.

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:36.360
And, like, nobody can see him or something.

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:53.180
That's it. Joe, it's funny that you mentioned, like, not doing anything, because that is, like, a popular YouTube series of video game videos where, like, you know, you try to win Mario Party by doing absolutely nothing.

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:58.220
Like, just letting everybody else make terrible decisions at the computers and winning.

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:03.280
And I think, you know, I don't doubt that John Elway's quarterback would do that.

00:28:03.440 --> 00:28:05.940
Again, I trampled the AI here.

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:14.440
But I'm sure, like, there's a lot of NES games that, you know, we could probably go back to and just the jankiness of them would let you win if you didn't really try.

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:17.300
Yeah, I'm sure that.

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:21.600
Shmups are like that, right? Shmups are like you just, you know, like you're kind of just dodging everything.

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:26.460
If you don't play, like, if you don't shoot along, you can make it through.

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:30.980
But there's still a level of, you know, engagement in that.

00:28:31.160 --> 00:28:34.740
Like, I feel like if you weren't dodging or shooting, you're probably going to die in a shmup.

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:38.420
I mean, I literally put the controller down and just watched.

00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:39.680
Yeah, well, that's true.

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:45.580
I just watched and, like, it's not like I did the thing where, like, okay, in some games you can, like, have the computer play the computer.

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:54.200
Like, I was playing the computer, but, like, the other players that I wasn't controlling were, like, their AI was good enough to beat the computer's terrible AI.

00:28:54.820 --> 00:28:56.340
That's some silly stuff right there.

00:28:57.860 --> 00:29:02.020
All right. Well, John Elway never made John Elway's Quarterback 2.

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:03.720
It doesn't exist.

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:04.860
There's no way to play it.

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:09.460
Don't read it in a magazine and be like, oh, I can't wait to get a hold of John Elway's Quarterback 2.

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:12.520
It never happened. Was there, like, were there ads for this?

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:19.560
No, but I'm just saying if you read about that, you know, if you pick up, like, if you go to a garage sale, pick up old issues of, you know, EGM.

00:29:20.060 --> 00:29:22.700
Don't be surprised if you're reading about it and you're like, oh, my God.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:23.680
Like, no, it didn't happen.

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:26.580
I know how many – I know how badly people wanted it.

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:31.800
John Elway would go on to be a Hall of Fame quarterback.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.720
And in 2004, he was inducted into the Hall of Fame.

00:29:35.320 --> 00:29:40.920
And, Sean, even though you might think he would go on to be the owner of the Denver Broncos –

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:42.800
He's the president of football operations.

00:29:43.220 --> 00:29:48.520
But wait, wait. He is the owner of four steakhouse restaurants, which I think is what you really meant.

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:49.780
That is true.

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:56.000
I forgot about Elway's Elbow Grease House.

00:29:56.440 --> 00:29:59.720
Wow. Well, you are right.

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:01.720
It's Elway's and then the name of the city.

00:30:02.080 --> 00:30:03.000
Oh, wow.

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:08.320
I'll say that I think on the fly, Elway's Elbow Grease is not a terrible name.

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:13.360
So, yeah, four successful steakhouse restaurants.

00:30:13.940 --> 00:30:22.520
And on January 5, 2011, John Elway was named the general manager and executive vice president of football operations of the Denver Broncos.

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:28.120
Apparently, according to Wikipedia, he has the final say in all football matters.

00:30:28.260 --> 00:30:31.440
I don't know how true that is if he's not the owner.

00:30:31.580 --> 00:30:38.180
Oh, yeah. Yeah, I mean, a lot of owners try and, like, take the reins and do all the things.

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:47.680
But some hire John Elway and let him just run it into the ground.

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:49.640
So that's sort of what happened over there.

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:59.560
I think he actually this year is actually, like, letting go of some control because he's been so bad at it outside of the Super Bowl that Peyton Manning won for him.

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:02.580
But I know I'm just talking to myself right now.

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:05.160
No, you've got it. You've got the very next bullet point.

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:13.900
As recently as January 4, 2021, John Elway stepped down as the general manager and is now just the president of the Broncos.

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:19.880
Yes. So it's been a quite a 10 year for him.

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:23.040
Yeah. I guess he likes his steaks well done.

00:31:23.300 --> 00:31:29.960
Ha! I don't have anything else to say about John Elway and there are no other sequels and spinoffs.

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:34.620
I think, you know, it's worth repeating that the arcade game sounds really cool for that spring loaded joystick.

00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:35.480
Yeah, I really want to play that.

00:31:36.500 --> 00:31:46.400
I would also like a game where it's like, you know that when you go to the arcades and there's those basketball things where you shoot real basketballs and the hoop moves?

00:31:46.400 --> 00:31:50.320
Kind of like it comes forward, it comes back, you know, like it's very long.

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:58.980
I would like a video game where you could actually pick up real footballs and throw them into the screen and then that would determine where you pass it.

00:31:59.000 --> 00:31:59.860
Like a golf dome.

00:32:00.860 --> 00:32:01.740
Yes, exactly.

00:32:01.960 --> 00:32:04.160
That'd be cool. I think it could be done.

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:11.400
I think there's a huge market in this day and age for giant arcades that take blocks of cities.

00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:14.620
I'm all for it.

00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:17.560
All right. We don't have much else to talk about here.

00:32:17.680 --> 00:32:27.600
So we are going to now decide if John Elway's quarterback is an essential game on a little thing we like to call the Essential Games List.

00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:40.200
Please remember when you're voting, you're voting about John Elway's quarterback, the video game, not John Elway's quarterback, whoever that might be.

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:44.000
Sean, with that in mind, please vote.

00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:53.180
I obviously I'll probably have more to more to gush about in sports games, whether they're good or bad.

00:32:54.380 --> 00:32:57.140
But I like this as a tech demo.

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:01.520
I like that they added a bit more nuance to play calling.

00:33:01.780 --> 00:33:06.940
And I really like the more analog nature of passing.

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:09.220
But everything else is unfinished.

00:33:09.220 --> 00:33:11.620
This game has zero presentation.

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:17.120
It's just a jank game.

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:21.420
So I cannot in good conscience even put this in the same breath as tech mode.

00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:22.880
So it's not essential.

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:24.320
All right. Sam?

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:28.340
Kind of the same stuff as what Sean was saying.

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:37.960
One thing we didn't touch on is that this is a rare game, which usually I feel like they're known for their presentation, at least from what we've seen.

00:33:38.260 --> 00:33:39.220
